recommendations.why:
Recyclers retype the same purchases and sales into each CPCB portal, and the portals reject back-dated rows and forbid edits. Exports should follow each portal's fields and date order:
- e-waste purchases by item code;
- tyre purchases (supplier, address, source, tonnes, invoice number, optional GSTIN, date);
- plastic sale e-invoice fields (GSTINs, IRN, HSN code, amount);
- battery quarterly Form 4 totals.

This saves hours each quarter and needs no CPCB API.

risks:
Partners' exposure: over-claiming leads to one of the following:
- a 1-year bar for plastic processors;
- e-waste registration revoked for up to 3 years;
- permanent revocation for used-oil recyclers on a third offence;
- prosecution under section 15 of the Environment Protection Act.
Any data-quality failure on Luma.Green becomes a partner's legal problem.

findings.detail:
G.S.R. 789(E) requires every e-commerce entity to show its legal name, headquarters and branch addresses, website, and customer-care and grievance-officer email, landline and mobile numbers. The grievance officer must acknowledge a complaint within 48 hours, give the complainant a copy as recorded and resolve it within one month. Joining the National Consumer Helpline's 'convergence' programme becomes mandatory. The amendment also requires:
- labels on sponsored listings;
- a 'prior price' (the lowest price in the previous 30 days) shown next to any discount;
- the seller's name on invoices in the same font size as the platform's name;
- a yearly dark-pattern self-audit, with the certificate displayed.
Marketplaces must show each seller's business name 'whether registered or not', address, customer-care number and rating, and explain what drives their rankings. Sellers must disclose any central ID such as a GSTIN or Udyam (MSME) number. DoCA's Lok Sabha answer of 25 Mar 2026 confirms the 2020 baseline (48 hours and one month), the NCH's 1915 number and 17 languages including Kannada, and the 13 dark patterns in the 2023 Guidelines.

MeitY's consolidated text (to 6 Apr 2023) requires Luma.Green to:
- publish rules, privacy policy and user agreement in English or an Eighth-Schedule language of the user's choice (Kannada, Hindi and Urdu are Eighth-Schedule languages; Arabic is not);
- remind users every year that breaking the terms can end their access;
- remove content within 36 hours of a court order or government notice;
- preserve removed content for 180 days, and keep registration data for 180 days after an account is closed;
- name a Grievance Officer who acknowledges complaints within 24 hours and resolves them within 15 days (72 hours for some removal requests).
Amendments made after April 2023 should be checked before the legal pages are final.

recommendations.why:
At least six of these numbers changed between Jul 2024 and Jan 2027:
- e-commerce TCS rate (10 Jul 2024);
- metal-scrap reverse charge and TDS (10 Oct 2024);
- 30-day self-invoice deadline (1 Nov 2024);
- TCS on scrap (1 Apr 2026);
- Legal Metrology penalties (1 May 2026);
- E-Commerce amendment (1 Jan 2027).
Keeping them as data meets the founders' 'easy to modify' requirement, and every other compliance feature reads from the same table.

findings.detail:
E-Waste (Management) Rules 2022, rule 12: makers and producers of PV modules, panels and cells must:
- register on the CPCB portal;
- store PV waste generated up to 2034-35 under CPCB guidelines;
- file annual returns until then;
- keep a separate inventory.

PV recyclers must meet CPCB material-recovery norms. PV is listed as category CEEW14 in Schedule I, and Schedules III and IV say recycling targets do not apply to PV waste. So panels earn no EPR certificates today. The value lies in collection logistics, safe storage, reuse testing, hand-off to registered recyclers, and chain-of-custody records that producers can show. KERC assumes a 25-year plant life, so bulk end-of-life is years away; near-term volume comes from breakage, failures and repowering.
